   {
    "label": "(c)(ii)",
    "marks": 6,
    "model": "Draw a large circular transverse section of a young root, with these features. Outer single layer of cells = epidermis, with a root hair growing out from one epidermal cell. Wide zone of large, thin-walled cells inside it = cortex. A ring of cells around the centre = endodermis. In the centre, xylem in a star or X shape with phloem in groups between its arms. Labels: dermal tissue (the epidermis, with the root hair), ground tissue (the cortex), vascular tissue (the xylem and phloem in the central core).",
    "model_source": "notes",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "diagram"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "draw-a-diagram",
    "offSpec": false,
    "notesRefs": [
     "bio30-s1"
    ]
   },
   {
    "label": "(c)(iii)",
    "marks": 6,
    "model": "Henry Dixon and John Joly.",
    "model_source": "notes",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "short-answer"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "none",
    "offSpec": false,
    "notesRefs": [
     "bio30-s2"
    ]
   },
   {
    "label": "(c)(iv)",
    "marks": 9,
    "model": "Transpiration: water evaporates from the mesophyll cell walls in the leaf and diffuses out through the stomata, and the mesophyll cells replace it from the xylem in the leaf veins. Tension: removing water from the top of the xylem pulls on the water column (transpiration pull), and this tension is passed down the stem to the roots, drawing water up and into the root hairs from the soil. Cohesion: water molecules stick to each other by hydrogen bonds, so the water forms an unbroken column in the xylem and each molecule pulled up drags the next one behind it. (Adhesion, the water sticking to the xylem walls, and root pressure, which pushes water up from below, also help.)",
    "model_source": "notes",
    "answerStatus": "written",
    "type": [
     "long-answer"
    ],
    "diagramRelation": "none",
    "offSpec": false,
    "notesRefs": [
     "bio30-s2"
    ]
   }
